**What changed (permission.go)**
- **Comments.** splitSegments and readDelimited track whether a word starts. A word starts at line start or after an unquoted, unescaped blank or operator. It does not start after a closed quote, ${…}, $(…), backquotes or an escaped blank. A '#' at a word start skips to the end of the line, and the comment is left out of the segment.
- **Line continuation.** A backslash-newline is removed outside single quotes: in splitSegments (unquoted and inside double quotes) and in shellTokens. This was a deny bypass on HEAD: `tou\<NL>ch M` and `"tou\<NL>ch" M` passed Bash(touch *). The card's double-quote rule names newline, so I applied the same rule to the unquoted case. That was my choice.
- **Backslash inside double quotes (shellTokens).** It is dropped only before $ ` " \ and stays before any other byte. This closes c1.
- **readBackquote(s, start, inDouble).** A backslash is dropped only before $ ` \, and before " when the backquotes stand in double quotes. bash 5.2 does the latter: "`echo \"x" ; touch M ; echo "y\"`" runs the touch.
- **Here-documents.** New: hereDoc, readHereDocWord, readHereDocs, namesShell.
  - `<<<` is a here-string; the word after it is ordinary.
  - `<<` and `<<-` read the delimiter. It is undecidable when there is no word, or when the word has `$`, a backquote, a backslash-newline, an unterminated quote or a backslash inside double quotes.
  - At the next unquoted newline, the bodies are skipped in order. Each line is compared whole with the delimiter; `<<-` strips leading tabs first, as bash does.
  - With an unquoted delimiter, a body holding `$(`, `${`, `$[`, a backquote or a line ending in a backslash is undecidable. bash joins `EO\<NL>F` into the delimiter. A plain `$VAR` is fine.
  - A delimiter line that never comes is undecidable, and so is a body that never starts.
  - Not on the card, added by me: if a segment on the operator's line names a shell (`bash <<EOF`, `<<EOF bash`, `env sh -s <<EOF`, `cat <<EOF | bash`), the body is split as a nested script. Otherwise "the body is data" would have let `bash <<'EOF'⏎touch M⏎EOF` pass the deny rule, and HEAD denied it.
- **readDelimited ($(…)).**
  - It now reads a `$(` inside double quotes as its own substitution, with its own quotes.
  - It handles comments and here-documents. A `(` or `)` starts a word.
  - It returns a "visible" text with comments and here-document bodies left out, and that text is what goes into the segment. outputRedirections reads the segment again after normalizeSpace has collapsed the newlines. With the comment or body still in it, the rescan would run past the `)` and miss `> out`. Both cases are tested and killed by M9.
